<p><em>There are biscuits on the sideboard if you want them.</em> That&#8217;s a natural-enough-sounding expression, but it&#8217;s also a peculiar thing to say.</p>
<p>&#8220;[T]he verb is the highly ordinary <em>are</em>,&#8221; <a href="https://archive.org/details/j.-l.-austin-philosophical-papers-edited-by-j.-o-urmson_202511/page/158/mode/2up">noted philosopher J.L. Austin</a> in 1956, &#8220;but the <em>if</em> is more like that in &#8216;I can if I choose&#8217; than that in &#8216;I panted if I ran&#8217;: for we can certainly infer from it that &#8216;There are biscuits on the sideboard whether you want them or not&#8217; and that anyway &#8216;There are biscuits on the sideboard&#8217;, whereas it would be folly to infer that &#8216;If there are no biscuits on the sideboard you do not want them&#8217;, or to understand the meaning to be that you have only to want biscuits to cause them to be on the sideboard.&#8221;</p>
<p>Delightfully, such expressions are called biscuit conditionals.</p>
